Since Budget 2027 was tabled on 9 October, one question keeps coming up from
Malaysian business owners:
_is there a Budget 2027 AI grant for SMEs, and can I apply yet?_
The short answer is yes, there's money set
aside for it, and no, you can't apply yet. The rules haven't been published.

That gap is actually useful. The businesses that get the most out of a
limited grant are usually the ones that worked out what they wanted to
automate before the form went live. Here's what has been announced, what
hasn't, and how to use the waiting time well.

## What Budget 2027 actually announced for AI and SMEs

The headline item for businesses is an allocation of
**RM30 million through the Malaysia Digital Economy Corporation (MDEC)** to
help around **4,000 micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs)** adopt artificial intelligence
and automate their operations
([The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/news/nation/2026/10/11/digital-ministry-pushes-ai-nation-goal-under-budget-2027),
[Malay Mail](https://www.malaymail.com/news/malaysia/2026/10/11/budget-2027-digital-ministry-targets-200000-ai-skilled-individuals-in-push-for-ai-nation-by-2030/238688)).

Two other AI measures made the headlines, and it's worth being clear that
neither is a business grant:

- **Tax relief on AI subscriptions.** The lifestyle relief for individuals is
  being broadened to include AI software subscriptions, within the existing
  lifestyle relief cap, from year of assessment 2027
  ([SoyaCincau](https://soyacincau.com/2026/10/09/budget-2027-ai-subscriptions-will-be-eligible-for-income-tax-relief/)).
  That's personal income tax relief, claimed by you as an individual, not
  funding for your company.
- **Free AI subscriptions for young people.** The government is adding
  100,000 free AI subscriptions and extending the programme to apps such as
  ChatGPT
  ([Twentytwo13](https://twentytwo13.my/budget-2027-tax-relief-for-ai-subscriptions-100000-more-free-subscriptions/)).
  Useful for staff skills, but again not a company grant.

The full text is in the
[Budget 2027 speech on the Ministry of Finance website](https://www.mof.gov.my/portal/en/news/speech/speech-the-fifth-madani-budget-2027),
and it's worth reading the relevant paragraphs yourself rather than relying on
summaries, including this one.

## What we don't know yet

As of mid-November, MDEC hasn't published guidelines for the RM30 million
fund. That means there's no confirmed answer to:

- **Who is eligible.** Turnover bands, sectors, years in operation and
  Malaysian ownership rules are all unannounced.
- **What form the help takes.** It could be a matching grant, a voucher,
  subsidised training, a programme with appointed vendors, or a mix.
- **How much each business gets.** RM30 million divided by 4,000 businesses
  is RM7,500 on average, but that's arithmetic, not a published amount. Malay
  Mail's
  [round-up of Budget 2027 measures](https://www.malaymail.com/news/malaysia/2026/11/06/free-ai-tax-breaks-and-business-grants-in-budget-2027-how-malaysians-stand-to-benefit/241696)
  cautions against reading the allocation as a fixed grant for every
  business.
- **When applications open**, and whether it's first come, first served.

If anyone tells you otherwise, ask them for the MDEC document they're quoting.
Watch [MDEC's own website](https://mdec.my) for the official announcement.

### What about the RM5,000 digital grant?

The closest precedent is the Geran Digital PMKS MADANI, which BSN ran with
MDEC: a 50% matching grant of up to RM5,000 on approved digital solutions
([BSN](https://www.bsn.com.my/page/MSMEMadani)). Its 2025 round is closed,
and reporting on the speech says Budget 2027 didn't reopen it
([Funding Societies](https://blog.fundingsocieties.com.my/budget-2027-malaysia/)).
The new AI fund may borrow some of its mechanics, such as approved vendors
and matching contributions, but nobody should assume its terms carry over
until MDEC says so.

## Why it's still worth preparing now

Four thousand places sounds like a lot until you compare it with the more
than 1.1 million businesses LHDN says are covered by its e-invoicing
exemption alone
([theSun](https://thesun.my/business/smes-msmes/msme-under-rm3m-exempt-e-invoicing/)). Matching grants in particular tend to reward businesses
that can show up with a clear use case, a quote and a vendor who knows the
paperwork. You don't need any of the rules to do that groundwork. You only
need to know your own business.

The other reason is less obvious: most of the value of AI for an SME doesn't
depend on the grant. If an automation only makes sense with a subsidy, it
probably isn't the right first project. If it makes sense without one, the
grant is a bonus that shortens the payback.

## Five AI projects that fit a typical Malaysian SME

These are the kinds of projects we see deliver results for small teams. Pick
the one that matches your biggest daily bottleneck.

### 1. Answering WhatsApp enquiries after hours

If your sales come through WhatsApp, every evening enquiry that waits until
morning is a lead at risk. An AI agent on your business number can reply in
the customer's language, ask the qualifying questions and hand over to a
person with the full thread. We've written about
[why businesses lose sales at 11pm](/blog/whatsapp-ai-agent-for-business)
and what an agent changes.

### 2. Lead capture into a CRM

Even without a chatbot, AI can summarise conversations and file the name,
need, budget and next step into a CRM, so nothing lives only in someone's
phone.

### 3. Document handling

Supplier invoices, delivery orders and claim forms are classic automation
targets: extract the fields, check them against a purchase order, flag the
mismatches for a human.

### 4. Internal knowledge answers

Price lists, SOPs, product specs and HR policies can be turned into an
internal assistant, so new staff stop interrupting the one person who knows
everything.

### 5. Reporting from the systems you already have

If your sales, stock and accounts already sit in one system, AI can draft the
weekly numbers and highlight what changed. If they don't, that's a sign the
foundation needs work first, which leads to the next point.

## Get your data foundation ready

AI is only as good as the data it can see. Before any grant opens, it's worth
checking:

- **Where your customer conversations live.** Personal phones and scattered
  WhatsApp numbers make automation hard.
- **Whether your records are digital and consistent.** Sales, stock and
  invoices in one system beat five spreadsheets.
- **Whether your e-invoicing is sorted.** LHDN's MyInvois data is becoming
  part of the wider picture. On 11 November MDEC signed seven financing
  partners for the National E-Invoice Financing Exchange (NEFX), which is
  being developed to use authenticated e-invoice data in SME credit
  assessments
  ([The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/business/business-news/2026/11/11/mdec-partners-seven-financial-institutions-to-boost-sme-financing-access)).
  If you haven't settled your approach yet, our
  [MyInvois guide for SMEs](/blog/myinvois-e-invoicing-guide-malaysian-smes)
  covers the options.

## A pre-application checklist

Use the waiting time to have these ready, so you can move the week the
guidelines land:

- SSM registration documents and your latest financial statements or
  management accounts
- Your annual turnover and headcount figures, since most SME schemes are
  tiered by size
- One clearly defined use case, written as a problem: "We miss most
  evening enquiries" beats "We want AI"
- A rough baseline: hours spent, enquiries missed or errors made today,
  so you can show the impact afterwards
- One or two vendor quotes with scope, setup cost and monthly cost
  separated
- A named person internally who owns the project after go-live
- Confirmation of who owns your data and how you'd export it if you
  switched vendors

## FAQ

### Can I apply for the Budget 2027 AI grant now?

No. As of mid-November 2026, MDEC hasn't published eligibility rules or an
application process for the RM30 million fund. Be wary of anyone taking
"pre-registrations" on its behalf.

### How much will each SME receive?

It hasn't been announced. The RM7,500 figure you may see is simply RM30
million divided by 4,000 businesses, not a confirmed grant amount.

### Can my company claim the AI subscription tax relief?

As reported, the relief is part of individual lifestyle relief, so it's
claimed by individuals on personal income tax, not by companies. Ask your tax
adviser how AI subscriptions are treated for your business.

### Should I wait for the grant before starting an AI project?

Only if the project doesn't make financial sense without it. If it does,
waiting costs you months of results, and grant schemes may not fund work
already started, so check the rules when they're published before signing
anything you hope to claim.

